[AGENCY NAME] maintains a written Emergency Disaster Plan on form LIC 610E (the RCFE version of the disaster plan form) as required by 22 CCR §87212. The plan is kept in a labeled Emergency Binder at [LOCATION OF BINDER] where every shift can reach it at any hour. It is also available to the licensing agency on request.

This policy meets LIC 281 item A14 — Emergency Disaster Plan (LIC 610D; for RCFE only, LIC 610E). The plan names two relocation sites away from the facility. Each site can accommodate every resident the facility is licensed to serve.

This policy meets LIC 281 item A14.1 — Authorization for relocation site. If [AGENCY NAME] does not itself occupy or own a relocation site, the agency or person in possession of the site gives written authorization to use it, and that authorization is verified in writing and kept with the plan.

The plan covers each element required by 22 CCR §87212:
(1) administrative authority and staff assignments;
(2) the fire safety plan (see EDP-02);
(3) means of exiting;
(4) assembly at a predetermined evacuation site;
(5) transportation;
(6) relocation sites equipped for safe temporary accommodation;
(7) supervision of residents during evacuation and contact with residents and responsible persons after relocation;
(8) contact with fire, law enforcement and disaster agencies; and
(9) notification of a resident's hospice agency when the resident is evacuated or relocated.

Emergency exit plans and emergency telephone numbers are posted in the facility at all times. The plan also serves as the facility's mass casualty plan.

This policy meets LIC 281 item B5.3 — Hiring practices, including screening of employees for necessary education and experience, and notice of the conditions of employment.

Screening for education and experience. Every applicant is screened against the job description in JD-01 for required age, education, certificates, training and experience. Anyone who will supervise staff or residents is at least 18 years old (22 CCR §87411).

Conditions of employment. Each applicant is informed in writing, before an offer is finalized, that employment is conditioned on:
(a) DOJ and FBI fingerprint criminal record clearance or exemption (22 CCR §87355), or a documented transfer of an existing clearance;
(b) a signed Statement of Criminal Convictions (LIC 508);
(c) tuberculosis clearance;
(d) a physician health screening, with a chest x-ray or intradermal test, completed within 6 months before or 7 days after employment, or a health questionnaire where licensing allows;
(e) any index check required by licensing, including a child abuse index check if one is required; and
(f) completion of orientation and required training (TR-01).

No one is employed in, or is present in, the facility until criminal record clearance, exemption or transfer is in place (22 CCR §87411, §87355). The applicant, the administrator, and every adult household member also need clearance. Screening is done lawfully and without discrimination in line with Title VII and the ADA: medical questions and health screening are made only after a conditional offer and apply equally to all employees in the same job.
